 {"q": "What does Hector decide when he realises Athena has tricked him?", "options": ["To run back into the city", "To beg Achilles for mercy", "To die fighting and do some great thing that will be remembered", "To curse the gods and refuse to fight"], "answer": 2, "why": "'Let me first do some great thing that shall be told among men hereafter': kleos is the only freedom left him."},
 {"q": "Achilles in session 2 said a life 'can neither be bought nor harried back'. How does his treatment of Hector contrast with that insight?", "options": ["It doesn't: he is consistent in valuing life above gifts", "Having once seen the value of life, he now refuses even to let a dead man's body be ransomed, and wishes to eat it: grief has made him less than human", "He now accepts the ransom he refused earlier", "He decides to go home after killing Hector"], "answer": 1, "why": "The man who saw the worth of a life now denies the dead any worth at all. The poem shows how far grief and rage have taken him."},
