   context="Priam has brought the body home. In Troy, the women lead the laments: first Andromache (her lament ends as this passage begins), then Hecuba, then Helen, the woman the war was fought over. 'Alexandrus' is Paris. Samos, Imbrus and Lemnos are islands where Achilles sold captured Trojans as slaves.",
   plain="Hecuba finishes her lament: Achilles dragged Hector round Patroclus's tomb, but the gods have kept his body fresh. Then Helen speaks: in twenty years at Troy, Hector never said an unkind word to her, and stopped others when they did. Now she has no friend left in Troy. Priam tells the people to bring wood, since Achilles has promised them a truce.",
